  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...    \nLate this week is shaping up to be a more active period for \nmonsoon thunderstorms across at least South-Central AZ. By \nThursday, anticyclonic subsidence will begin to diminish as the \nsubtropical high weakens overhead, and abundant low level moisture\nwill remain in place through the rest of the week and linger into\nthe weekend. Latest NBM guidance seems to favor Friday with the \nbest chances for lower desert thunderstorms, which makes sense \ngiven the weaker subsidence aloft and plenty of moisture and \ninstability to support numerous thunderstorms over the high \nterrain by the afternoon, sending outflows into the lower deserts \nand triggering additional thunderstorms by the evening. GFS bufr \nsoundings indicate a good balance between CAPE and DCAPE Thursday \nevening, with values between 1000-1500 J/kg, which is typically a \ngood indicator for severe weather potential over the Valley. \nHowever, CIN may be a bit stronger than preferred, and the \ndevelopment of thunderstorms that can utilize this environment \nwill be entirely dependent on the presence and timing of a \nsufficient lifting mechanism (i.e., thunderstorm outflow \nboundary). This conditional threat continues Friday, but with \nperhaps lower DCAPE and shallower inverted-V soundings. Strong \nthunderstorm winds, dense blowing dust, locally heavy rainfall \nresulting in instances of flash flooding, and frequent lightning \nwill all be on the table for thunderstorm hazards later this week.\n\nThe forecast for this weekend into early next week is much less \ncertain, however, a few more details are coming into focus tonight\ncompared to the guidance available in previous forecast updates. \nEnsembles are in better agreement that mean troughing will \nestablish over the Northwest US, pushing the northern stream and \nlikely a series of upper lows and their attendant jet streaks as \nfar south as the Great Basin. WPC cluster analysis reveals \ndiscrepancies on how far south the mean troughing will establish \nand how strongly negative the midlevel height anomalies will \nbecome over the Northwest US. The current consensus is for flow \naloft to shift south to southwesterly as a result, meaning the \nmore typical monsoonal pattern which briefly sets up this week \nwill be short-lived, becoming something more akin to a transition \nseason setup. This does not, however, mean that thunderstorm \nchances leave the forecast. Drying will begin to occur from west \nto east, but low level moisture will not be scoured out from \nSouthern AZ. Thunderstorms will likely continue to favor \ndevelopment over prominent terrain features, and lower desert \nstorm chances will be dependent on outflows or a nearly uncapped \nenvironment. As for now, NBM/WPC PoPs have come in lower this \nweekend compared to Thu/Fri, and timing of the better chances \nseems a bit unrealistic, reflecting the high uncertainty and \nnebulous forcing mechanisms. Regardless, the main message for this\nweekend into early next week will be continued thunderstorm \nchances over at least South-Central AZ higher terrain areas, but \nlikely a decrease in activity from Thu/Fri due to the pattern \nshift and until southeasterly flow returns. \n\nWith the subtropical high diminished and then displaced by \nNorthwest US troughing this weekend, temperatures will follow a \ndownward trend, attaining near normal values likely by Saturday or\nSunday, ending the extreme heat conditions.",
